  "summary": "The throwing-generator path in packages/cli/src/lint/metadata-eval.ts no longer substitutes an empty stack and scores it; both failure paths now take the same unscorableScore() verdict (0 / F / valid: false), so a case that produced nothing contributes 0 to meanScore instead of the 100 an empty stack borrows. passed / ok / failed / exit codes are untouched and are asserted UNCHANGED in both new test legs, per Zone 1 clause 2. The denominator fork the dispatch handed over is DECIDED, not deferred: the mean stays over cases ATTEMPTED, and the reason is now written into the payload's own doc comment. Route 2 alone (dropping failures from the denominator) was rejected on measurement, because it leaves the wrong per-case score in results[i].score, which is the value the card actually names. All four PM assumptions were re-driven: A2.1 CONFIRMED and re-derived at my own base (origin/main moved f5cc78b63f3 to d30ccb9bd96 between dispatch and start; no commit in that window touched packages/cli at all, so the seat's held-by-nobody reading stands rather than being carried over). A2.2 CONFIRMED (unscorableScore() is on main, both paths in one module, located by content). A2.3 CONFIRMED by re-driving it myself rather than taking the card's word: scoreMetadata({}) is {score:100, grade:A, valid:true, counts all zero}, driven twice, once on the module through tsx and once end to end through the CLI's source entry. A2.4 CONFIRMED (the reducer sums r.score.score with no generationError filter). A2.5 is PARTLY FALSIFIED and I declare Clause-2: YES against the seat's provisional no: its mechanical half is exactly right and I measured it (0 keys added, 0 removed, 25 payload key paths before and after), but the not-mechanizable conformance limb catches this change, because it re-selects an input class between two ALREADY-PUBLISHED verdicts on a shipped command's --json face, and the doctrine for that class is grade yes when the call is not clear. needs:contract-review is hung on card and PR together, in the same round, with contrastive read-backs on both carriers; nothing was pre-hung. TWO PROCESS NOTES for the seat: (1) Zone 1 clause 8 says assign yourself, the standing os-dev contract says the assignee belongs to the dispatching PM seat and the branch is the identity bit under the shared account. I followed the standing contract, did NOT set the assignee, and said so in the claim comment. (2) The card arrived labelled pm:dispatched with no assignee set, which is the PM-side half-state the re-dispatch comment describes; I started work rather than stopping, as instructed.",
  "tests": "All green at the final commit dac629d195b, the head of the branch; every exit code captured after redirection to a file, never through a pipe. (1) GATE UNION re-derived from the delivered diff with `node scripts/pm/dispatch-gates.mjs --repo objectstack-ai/objectstack` at dac629d195b: 4 paths, NO STALE TREE banner, Reconciliation line = 56 runnable families (45 by path + 6 by change kind + 7 declared whole-tree, 2 reached both ways). 56 of 56 RUN, 56 of 56 GREEN. Three i18n families first answered `exit 3 PREREQUISITE NOT MET` on an unbuilt CLI closure and were recorded as NOT MEASURED, never as red; the closure was built and all three then answered 0 (`check-i18n-coverage: OK (13 configs, 621 baselined untranslated strings, none new)`). check:dual-build-cjs-loads did the same and then passed 103 published require entry points across 66 packages. check:type-check-debt: `13 ledger entries re-measured in 98.7s, 143 raw tsc errors total, none above its recorded number`. (2) `pnpm lint` -- the WHOLE-REPO eslint scan -- was run in full, exit 0, so there is no narrowing to declare on lint at all. (3) `pnpm --filter @objectstack/cli typecheck` exit 0, including check:test-typecheck: `OK -- @objectstack/cli's test layer compiles under packages/cli/tsconfig.test.json; 3 files / 28 errors / 6 pinned signatures held`. NOT-MEASURED trap checked rather than assumed: all three edited files were confirmed inside a tsc program with --listFiles, 1 hit each, so `typecheck clean` really does cover this diff. (4) TESTS: `pnpm --filter @objectstack/cli exec vitest run --maxWorkers=2 test/metadata-eval.test.ts test/lint-eval-json-unscorable-stack.e2e.test.ts` -> `Test Files 2 passed (2) · Tests 29 passed (29)`, under the shared verify lock (VERDICT command-exit 0). (5) END-TO-END BEFORE/AFTER on the published command shape, same generator, same tree: BEFORE `exit 1 · ok:false · passed:0 · failed:5 · meanScore:100`, every case score 100 / grade A / valid true / generationError 'model unavailable'; AFTER `exit 1 · ok:false · passed:0 · failed:5 · meanScore:0`, every case 0 / F / valid false. (6) ABLATION -- direction predicted IN WRITING FIRST (RED, and the five failing tests named in advance), then measured: `Test Files 2 failed (2) · Tests 5 failed | 24 passed (29)`, exactly the five predicted and no others, every failure reading `AssertionError: expected 100 to be +0`. REBUILD: none needed and none done, and the reason is stated rather than assumed -- both legs load the mutated file from SOURCE (the unit test imports ../src/lint/metadata-eval by relative path; the e2e drives packages/cli/bin/run-dev.js, whose own header calls itself the SOURCE entry point that exists so suites do not depend on packages/cli/dist), so no dist sits on the measured path for this file; its dependency @objectstack/spec does resolve through exports to dist and is untouched by this diff, and that closure was built before the baseline. MUTATION PROVEN ON DISK, not by an editor's exit code: removed-text counts 1 -> 0 on both anchors, injected-marker counts 0 -> 1 on both (ABLATION_MARKER and the restored `stack = {};`), blob hash f7b4ac2a1bb -> 53b31d9e211. RESTORE PROVEN, not trapped-and-hoped: `git checkout HEAD -- ABSOLUTE_PATH` under `trap ... EXIT INT TERM`, then blob-hash equality with the HEAD blob (f7b4ac2a1bb both sides, non-empty -- an empty hash would have been a FAILURE) AND `git diff HEAD` at 0 bytes. The first ablation attempt returned the lock's exit 99 (queue-timeout, NOT MEASURED, recorded as neither pass nor red) and the trap restored the tree for real; the retry on the kept slot produced the numbers above. ONE DECLARED NARROWING: the package's full `pnpm test` was NOT run. packages/cli's own recorded suite cost is 185 files / 2115 tests / 793.31s on a quiet box, against this container's ~10-minute foreground ceiling on a shared machine. The blast radius was measured instead of guessed: the only runtime importer of metadata-eval.ts is src/commands/lint.ts (driven end to end by the e2e leg), src/lint/corpus.ts imports the MetadataEvalCase TYPE only and that interface's shape is unchanged, src/lint/score.ts mentions it in a comment, and no other test file in the package names runMetadataEval, `lint --eval` or meanScore. CI runs the sharded farm regardless. NOT MEASURED, recorded as such: `node scripts/pm/check-clause2-carriers.mjs --pair 15659` answered exit 3 PREREQUISITE NOT MET (repo-scoped REST is 403 from this container, 0 pairs read) -- the two contrastive label read-backs are what this seat can attest to instead.",
  "mcp_calls": "17 -- MCP GitHub calls for the whole run. Repo-scoped REST answers 403 in this container and gh is absent, so card body and all pre-existing comments were read through the zero-quota embedded-JSON payload on the public issue page, and only writes, one dedup search with its positive control, and the label/body read-backs went through MCP.",
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "question": "Clause 2 grade: I declared YES and it overturns the dispatching seat's provisional NO (A2.5). Does the seat accept the overturn, or restore its own reading?",
      "options": [
        "A -- accept YES: the change re-selects an input class between two already-published verdicts (100/A/valid vs 0/F/invalid) on a shipped command's --json face, which is the not-mechanizable conformance limb, and the doctrine for that limb is grade yes when the call is not clear (false yes self-corrects in a review round, false no ships). The card's filing seat and triage both predicted clause 2 independently, before any diff existed.",
        "B -- restore NO: the mechanical floor is genuinely not triggered, and I measured it rather than assuming it (25 payload key paths before, 25 after, 0 added, 0 removed); nothing a caller can send is newly accepted or rejected, no exported symbol is added, and the value only moves on runs that already reported ok:false."
      ],
      "recommendation": "A. The label is already hung on both carriers and the PR is draft, so A costs one review round and B costs nothing to reach from A -- while the reverse (shipping under a wrong NO) is exactly the failure the doctrine names. B's evidence is preserved verbatim in the PR body and in the clause-2 comment on the card so the seat can act on it without re-deriving anything."
    },
    {
      "question": "meanScore's denominator: mean over cases ATTEMPTED (failures counted as 0) or over SCORABLE cases only, with the failure count reported separately? Decided here rather than deferred; flagged so the seat can overrule with the reason visible.",
      "options": [
        "A -- over ATTEMPTED (what I shipped): the denominator is unchanged, the metric keeps its documented meaning, and a failed case contributes its 0. Pinned by a test that also fails if anyone later switches to the scorable-only mean without saying so.",
        "B -- over SCORABLE only: reports the quality of the generations that arrived, but says nothing about how many never did, and the two answers differ whenever only SOME generations threw."
      ],
      "recommendation": "A, and B alone does not even repair this card: the value the card names as wrong is the score stamped on the FAILED case (100 / A / valid: true for a case that produced nothing), which survives untouched if you merely drop that case from a mean. A fixes the per-case lie and the mean together. Consumers were measured before choosing: in-repo there are exactly two, the human summary line in commands/lint.ts (`mean N/100`) and the --json payload itself; neither filters or re-derives, so nothing depended on the old denominator. Route B remains available as its own card if the seat wants a scorable-only statistic, but the card's own words are that a silently switched denominator is a worse defect than the one being fixed."
    },
    {
      "question": "Process conflict, reported rather than silently resolved: Zone 1 clause 8 of the dispatch says to assign myself; the standing os-dev contract says the assignee field belongs to the dispatching PM seat and that the branch, not the assignee, is the identity bit under the shared account.",
      "options": [
        "A -- follow the standing contract: claim by comment plus pushed branch, leave the assignee to the seat (what I did).",
        "B -- follow Zone 1 clause 8 and self-assign."
      ],
      "recommendation": "A. The standing contract is the unconditional clause and wins on conflict; the previous dispatch of this same card resolved it the same way. The card is currently pm:dispatched with the assignee cleared -- a PM-side half-state the seat may want to close now that work has landed."
    }
  ],
  "out_of_scope_findings": [
    "filed as #15658: scoreMetadata swallows a lintConfig crash into issues: [], so a schema-valid stack whose linter never ran scores 100 / A / valid: true -- byte-identical to a perfect one, with no key recording that half the rubric did not run. Same family as this card one module down (packages/cli/src/lint/score.ts), but wider: it reaches os lint --score as well as the eval, and unlike this card it can turn a failure into a PASS rather than only into a wrong mean, because that path sets no generationError. Filed unassigned and bare, with its reachability stated as the open question a triager should settle first (no input was constructed that makes lintConfig throw after normalizeStackInput and safeParse have already walked the value). Deduped with one targeted search whose positive control returned #15578 in the same session, so the empty result is a reading and not a silent zero."
  ]
